"use strict";
window.LC_CHUNKS=window.LC_CHUNKS||{};window.LC_CHUNKS["university_data-science-and-artificial-intelligence_en"]=window.LC_CHUNKS["university_data-science-and-artificial-intelligence_en"]||{};window.LC_CHUNKS["university_data-science-and-artificial-intelligence_en"][13]={"university_data-science-and-artificial-intelligence_en:M13-L1":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L1\">M13-L1 – State spaces, problem formulation and search graphs<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M13 – Bachelor: Symbolic AI and intelligent search (8 lessons)<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Bachelor · Core/elective<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prerequisites:<\/strong> M2, M3.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Learning objective:<\/strong> Model a discrete problem as a\nstate space and determine admissible paths and their costs.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>From the task to the state model<\/h2>\n<p>A mobile transport cart must travel from a storage room to a packing\nstation. A task is not yet a search problem: first we must decide which\ninformation determines the possible actions. If all passages are open\nand the cart always carries the same load, its location is sufficient.\nIf a key must be collected on the way, both location and possession of\nthe key are necessary. Two situations with the same position but\ndifferent keys must not be treated as identical. A state model is useful\nwhen the available actions, their consequences and the goal test can all\nbe determined from the modelled state alone.<\/p>\n<p>Formally, let\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>S<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">S<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nbe the set of states,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>s<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><mo>∈<\/mo><mi>S<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">s_0\\in S<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nthe initial state,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>A<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>s<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">A(s)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nthe set of admissible actions,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>T<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>s<\/mi><mo>,<\/mo><mi>a<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">T(s,a)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nthe successor state and\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>G<\/mi><mo>⊆<\/mo><mi>S<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">G\\subseteq S<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nthe goal set. In this module we initially consider known, deterministic\ntransitions. The cost function\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>c<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>s<\/mi><mo>,<\/mo><mi>a<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">c(s,a)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nassigns each action a non-negative value; costs might be minutes or\nunits of energy. Different units must not be added without defined\nweighting.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Graph, path and search tree<\/h2>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-university-data-science-and-artificial-intelligence-en/media/img/001739_en.svg\"\nalt=\"Vier Zustände mit fünf gerichteten Kanten; zwei Wege kosten 5 Minuten, einer 8 Minuten.\" />\n<figcaption aria-hidden=\"true\">Vier Zustände mit fünf gerichteten\nKanten; zwei Wege kosten 5 Minuten, einer 8 Minuten.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p><em>Figure: Zustandsgraph und Wegkosten.<\/em><\/p>\n<p>An original teaching graph consists of locations S, A, B and Z. It\nhas directed connections S→A (2), S→B (1), A→Z (3), B→A (1) and B→Z (7).\nThe numbers denote minutes. The illustration shows that several paths\ncan lead to the same state. In contrast, a search tree contains paths as\nnodes and may list A several times. A return edge would even allow\narbitrarily long repetitions in the search tree. Graph search therefore\nstores information about states already reached; cost-based search may\nneed to take account of a newly discovered cheaper path.<\/p>\n<p>For a path\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>p<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><msub><mi>s<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><mo>,<\/mo><msub><mi>a<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><mo>,<\/mo><msub><mi>s<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/msub><mo>,<\/mo><mi>…<\/mi><mo>,<\/mo><msub><mi>a<\/mi><mrow><mi>k<\/mi><mo>−<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>,<\/mo><msub><mi>s<\/mi><mi>k<\/mi><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">p=(s_0,a_0,s_1,\\ldots,a_{k-1},s_k)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nthe cost is<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>C<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>p<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><munderover><mo>∑<\/mo><mrow><mi>i<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><mrow><mi>k<\/mi><mo>−<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><\/munderover><mi>c<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><msub><mi>s<\/mi><mi>i<\/mi><\/msub><mo>,<\/mo><msub><mi>a<\/mi><mi>i<\/mi><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">C(p)=\\sum_{i=0}^{k-1}c(s_i,a_i).<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>S→A→Z costs 5 minutes, S→B→A→Z also costs 5, and S→B→Z costs 8.\nReaching a goal is not the same as finding a least-cost goal. If several\nsolutions are equally good, a search algorithm may return any of them\nunless a secondary condition is specified.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Checking the model in practice<\/h2>\n<p>The following small enumeration serves only to check this acyclic\nteaching graph. Without cycle protection it would not be a general graph\nsearch.<\/p>\n<div class=\"sourceCode\" id=\"cb1\"><pre\nclass=\"sourceCode python\"><code class=\"sourceCode python\"><span id=\"cb1-1\"><a href=\"#cb1-1\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>graph <span class=\"op\">=<\/span> {<span class=\"st\">&#39;S&#39;<\/span>:[(<span class=\"st\">&#39;A&#39;<\/span>,<span class=\"dv\">2<\/span>),(<span class=\"st\">&#39;B&#39;<\/span>,<span class=\"dv\">1<\/span>)], <span class=\"st\">&#39;A&#39;<\/span>:[(<span class=\"st\">&#39;Z&#39;<\/span>,<span class=\"dv\">3<\/span>)],<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-2\"><a href=\"#cb1-2\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>         <span class=\"st\">&#39;B&#39;<\/span>:[(<span class=\"st\">&#39;A&#39;<\/span>,<span class=\"dv\">1<\/span>),(<span class=\"st\">&#39;Z&#39;<\/span>,<span class=\"dv\">7<\/span>)], <span class=\"st\">&#39;Z&#39;<\/span>:[]}<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-3\"><a href=\"#cb1-3\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"kw\">def<\/span> paths(s):<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-4\"><a href=\"#cb1-4\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>    <span class=\"cf\">if<\/span> s <span class=\"op\">==<\/span> <span class=\"st\">&#39;Z&#39;<\/span>:<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-5\"><a href=\"#cb1-5\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>        <span class=\"cf\">return<\/span> [((<span class=\"st\">&#39;Z&#39;<\/span>,),<span class=\"dv\">0<\/span>)]<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-6\"><a href=\"#cb1-6\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>    <span class=\"cf\">return<\/span> [((s,)<span class=\"op\">+<\/span>p,c<span class=\"op\">+<\/span>d) <span class=\"cf\">for<\/span> t,c <span class=\"kw\">in<\/span> graph[s] <span class=\"cf\">for<\/span> p,d <span class=\"kw\">in<\/span> paths(t)]<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-7\"><a href=\"#cb1-7\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"cf\">assert<\/span> <span class=\"bu\">sorted<\/span>(c <span class=\"cf\">for<\/span> p,c <span class=\"kw\">in<\/span> paths(<span class=\"st\">&#39;S&#39;<\/span>)) <span class=\"op\">==<\/span> [<span class=\"dv\">5<\/span>,<span class=\"dv\">5<\/span>,<span class=\"dv\">8<\/span>]<\/span><\/code><\/pre><\/div>\n<p>In real problems the graph is often not stored in advance: a\nsuccessor generator constructs only the necessary parts. With\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>d<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">d<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nindependent binary state features, up to\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msup><mn>2<\/mn><mi>d<\/mi><\/msup><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">2^d<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ndifferent combinations are possible. The modelling therefore directly\ndetermines the size of the search. Unnecessary features enlarge the\nspace, whereas missing features make results unreliable. Tests of\npermitted, forbidden and boundary-case actions check whether the\nabstraction actually represents the task.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Exercise and solution<\/h2>\n<p>An additional door from A to B costs 1 minute. Why is the recursive\nfunction above no longer sufficient? Solution: it can follow A→B→A\nindefinitely. With strictly positive costs, a shortest path can be\nchosen without such cycles. What information must be added if B is\naccessible only to an empty cart? The load status must be included in\nthe state description. <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_data-science-and-artificial-intelligence&amp;module=M13&amp;lesson=L2&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M13-L2<\/a>\ndiscusses methods that do not need to enumerate every path.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources and further reading<\/h2>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://www.cs.ubc.ca/~poole/aibook/3e/html/ArtInt3e.Ch3.html\">Poole/Mackworth\n(2023): Searching for Solutions<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> – chapter overview, state space;\nselective cross-check. <strong>Verification:<\/strong> pending.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>","university_data-science-and-artificial-intelligence_en:M13-L2":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L2\">M13-L2 – Uninformed search, costs and heuristics<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M13 – Bachelor: Symbolic AI and intelligent search (8 lessons)<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Bachelor · Core/elective<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prerequisites:<\/strong> M2, M3, <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_data-science-and-artificial-intelligence&amp;module=M13&amp;lesson=L1&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M13-L1<\/a>.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Learning objective:<\/strong> Select breadth-first,\ndepth-first and uniform-cost search according to the goal and costs.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Ordering the open paths<\/h2>\n<p>A search routine maintains a set of possibilities still to be\nexamined, the frontier. Many methods differ in the criterion used to\nselect the next entry. Uninformed here means that no estimate of the\nremaining distance to the goal is used. The method nevertheless knows\nthe transitions, the goal criterion and, where relevant, the costs\nincurred so far.<\/p>\n<p>Breadth-first search (BFS) uses a queue: entries added first are\nexamined first. Depth-first search (DFS) behaves like a stack and\nfollows the most recently added branch. In a finite graph with\nvisited-state marking, both can find reachable goals. In an infinite\nsearch space, however, DFS may become stuck in an endless branch. BFS is\ncomplete with a finite branching factor and a goal at finite depth, but\norders paths only by their number of steps, not by differing costs.\nWithout additional conditions, DFS is optimal neither in cost nor in\ndepth. Iterative deepening repeats the search with increasing depth\nlimits and combines low tree-search memory requirements with\ncompleteness at a finite branching factor.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Few steps do not always mean low cost<\/h2>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-university-data-science-and-artificial-intelligence-en/media/img/001533_en.svg\"\nalt=\"Breitensuche wählt die direkte Kante mit Kosten 9, Kostensuche die zwei Kanten mit Gesamtkosten 4.\" />\n<figcaption aria-hidden=\"true\">Breitensuche wählt die direkte Kante mit\nKosten 9, Kostensuche die zwei Kanten mit Gesamtkosten 4.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p><em>Figure: Breitensuche und Kostensuche.<\/em><\/p>\n<p>A test device can connect S directly to Z (9 cost units), or via A\n(2) and then Z (2). BFS prefers the direct route; uniform-cost search\n(UCS) finds the cost of 4. UCS removes the path with the lowest cost\nincurred so far,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>g<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>s<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">g(s)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nWith non-negative edge costs in finite graphs, a state may be closed\nwhen its best entry is removed. For infinite graphs, a general\ncompleteness guarantee requires additional conditions, such as a finite\nbranching factor and a strictly positive lower bound on costs. The\nillustration compares the priorities.<\/p>\n<div class=\"sourceCode\" id=\"cb1\"><pre\nclass=\"sourceCode python\"><code class=\"sourceCode python\"><span id=\"cb1-1\"><a href=\"#cb1-1\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"im\">from<\/span> heapq <span class=\"im\">import<\/span> heappop, heappush<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-2\"><a href=\"#cb1-2\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>graph <span class=\"op\">=<\/span> {<span class=\"st\">&#39;S&#39;<\/span>:[(<span class=\"st\">&#39;A&#39;<\/span>,<span class=\"dv\">2<\/span>),(<span class=\"st\">&#39;Z&#39;<\/span>,<span class=\"dv\">9<\/span>)],<span class=\"st\">&#39;A&#39;<\/span>:[(<span class=\"st\">&#39;Z&#39;<\/span>,<span class=\"dv\">2<\/span>)],<span class=\"st\">&#39;Z&#39;<\/span>:[]}<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-3\"><a href=\"#cb1-3\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>q <span class=\"op\">=<\/span> [(<span class=\"dv\">0<\/span>,<span class=\"st\">&#39;S&#39;<\/span>)]<span class=\"op\">;<\/span> best <span class=\"op\">=<\/span> {<span class=\"st\">&#39;S&#39;<\/span>:<span class=\"dv\">0<\/span>}<span class=\"op\">;<\/span> order <span class=\"op\">=<\/span> []<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-4\"><a href=\"#cb1-4\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"cf\">while<\/span> q:<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-5\"><a href=\"#cb1-5\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>    g,s <span class=\"op\">=<\/span> heappop(q)<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-6\"><a href=\"#cb1-6\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>    <span class=\"cf\">if<\/span> g <span class=\"op\">!=<\/span> best[s]:<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-7\"><a href=\"#cb1-7\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>        <span class=\"cf\">continue<\/span><\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-8\"><a href=\"#cb1-8\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>    order.append(s)<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-9\"><a href=\"#cb1-9\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>    <span class=\"cf\">if<\/span> s <span class=\"op\">==<\/span> <span class=\"st\">&#39;Z&#39;<\/span>:<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-10\"><a href=\"#cb1-10\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>        <span class=\"cf\">break<\/span><\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-11\"><a href=\"#cb1-11\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>    <span class=\"cf\">for<\/span> t,c <span class=\"kw\">in<\/span> graph[s]:<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-12\"><a href=\"#cb1-12\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>        <span class=\"cf\">if<\/span> g<span class=\"op\">+<\/span>c <span class=\"op\">&lt;<\/span> best.get(t,<span class=\"bu\">float<\/span>(<span class=\"st\">&#39;inf&#39;<\/span>)):<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-13\"><a href=\"#cb1-13\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>            best[t] <span class=\"op\">=<\/span> g<span class=\"op\">+<\/span>c<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-14\"><a href=\"#cb1-14\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>            heappush(q,(g<span class=\"op\">+<\/span>c,t))<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-15\"><a href=\"#cb1-15\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"cf\">assert<\/span> (g,order) <span class=\"op\">==<\/span> (<span class=\"dv\">4<\/span>,[<span class=\"st\">&#39;S&#39;<\/span>,<span class=\"st\">&#39;A&#39;<\/span>,<span class=\"st\">&#39;Z&#39;<\/span>])<\/span><\/code><\/pre><\/div>\n<p>The first goal path discovered during expansion might be the\nexpensive one. UCS therefore tests for the goal when removing an entry.\nThe old entry with cost 9 remains in the queue but is invalidated by the\nbetter value.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Resource requirements and introductory heuristics<\/h2>\n<p>For a regular search tree with branching factor\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>b<\/mi><mo>&gt;<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">b&gt;1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\na complete traversal down to depth\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>d<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">d<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ngenerates\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>1<\/mn><mo>+<\/mo><mi>b<\/mi><mo>+<\/mo><mi>…<\/mi><mo>+<\/mo><msup><mi>b<\/mi><mi>d<\/mi><\/msup><mo>=<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><msup><mi>b<\/mi><mrow><mi>d<\/mi><mo>+<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msup><mo>−<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mi>/<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>b<\/mi><mo>−<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">1+b+\\ldots+b^d=(b^{d+1}-1)/(b-1)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nnodes; the actual frontier may depend on the order and early\ntermination. Even with\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>b<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>3<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mi>d<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>5<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">b=3,d=5<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nthe entire tree contains 364 nodes. A heuristic\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>h<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>s<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">h(s)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ninstead attempts to estimate the remaining cost from the state\ndescription. Greedy best-first search selects only the smallest\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>h<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">h<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nwithout accounting for the distance already travelled. A seemingly\nnearby goal behind a barrier may thus require expensive detours. UCS and\nthe A* search discussed in <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_data-science-and-artificial-intelligence&amp;module=M13&amp;lesson=L3&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M13-L3<\/a>\naccount for the costs already incurred.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Exercise and solution<\/h2>\n<p>The direct route now costs 3, while the other two edges still cost 2\neach. What do BFS and UCS return? Solution: both find the direct route\ncosting 3. UCS first examines A at cost 2 but identifies that the route\nvia A costs 4 in total. With a negative-cost edge, the closing steps of\nthe algorithm would no longer be generally correct.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources and further reading<\/h2>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://www.cs.ubc.ca/~poole/aibook/3e/html/ArtInt3e.Ch3.S5.html\">Poole/Mackworth\n(2023): Uninformed Search Strategies<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> – breadth-first, depth-first\nand uniform-cost search; selective cross-check.\n<strong>Verification:<\/strong> pending.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://www.cs.ubc.ca/~poole/aibook/3e/html/ArtInt3e.Ch3.S6.html\">Poole/Mackworth\n(2023): Informed (Heuristic) Search<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> – heuristics and A*; selective\ncross-check. <strong>Verification:<\/strong> pending.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>","university_data-science-and-artificial-intelligence_en:M13-L3":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"m13-l3-a-admissibility-and-consistency-l3\">M13-L3 – A*, admissibility and consistency<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M13 – Bachelor: Symbolic AI and intelligent search (8 lessons)<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Bachelor · Core/elective<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prerequisites:<\/strong> M2, M3, <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_data-science-and-artificial-intelligence&amp;module=M13&amp;lesson=L2&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M13-L2<\/a>.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Learning objective:<\/strong> Calculate A*, distinguish\nadmissible from consistent heuristics and explain the role of\nre-expansion.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Priority based on cost and prediction<\/h2>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-university-data-science-and-artificial-intelligence-en/media/img/001500_en.svg\"\nalt=\"A* wählt nach S den Zustand B mit f=5 vor A mit f=6 und erreicht Z mit Kosten 5.\" />\n<figcaption aria-hidden=\"true\">A* wählt nach S den Zustand B mit f=5 vor\nA mit f=6 und erreicht Z mit Kosten 5.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p><em>Figure: A<\/em>: Aufwand und Restkostenschätzung.*<\/p>\n<p>A* search combines the actual distance from the start\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>g<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>s<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">g(s)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nalong the best known path so far with an estimated remaining distance\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>h<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>s<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">h(s)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nSelection is based on<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>f<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>s<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mi>g<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>s<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>+<\/mo><mi>h<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>s<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">f(s)=g(s)+h(s).<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>We use a small transport graph with edges S→A (2), S→B (3), A→Z (5),\nB→Z (2). Set\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>h<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>S<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mn>4<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mi>h<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>A<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mn>4<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mi>h<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>B<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mi>h<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>Z<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">h(S)=4,h(A)=4,h(B)=2,h(Z)=0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe estimates have the same unit as the edge costs. After S, A with\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>f<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>6<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">f=6<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand B with\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>f<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>5<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">f=5<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nare available. B is expanded before A and yields Z with\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>g<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>f<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>5<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">g=f=5<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe illustration presents costs and estimates together. Search\nprioritised exclusively by\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>g<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">g<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nwould examine A before B.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Admissibility, consistency and optimality<\/h2>\n<p>Let\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>h<\/mi><mo>*<\/mo><\/msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>s<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">h^*(s)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nbe the minimum remaining cost from\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>s<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">s<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nto a goal. A non-negative heuristic is admissible if\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>h<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>s<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>≤<\/mo><msup><mi>h<\/mi><mo>*<\/mo><\/msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>s<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">h(s)\\le h^*(s)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nholds for every reachable state. Consistency additionally requires, for\neach edge with cost\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>c<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>s<\/mi><mo>,<\/mo><mi>t<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">c(s,t)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>h<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>s<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>≤<\/mo><mi>c<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>s<\/mi><mo>,<\/mo><mi>t<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>+<\/mo><mi>h<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>t<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>,<\/mo><mspace width=\"2.0em\"><\/mspace><mi>h<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>z<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">h(s)\\le c(s,t)+h(t),\\qquad h(z)=0.<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>Summing these inequalities along any goal path gives\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>h<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>s<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>≤<\/mo><mi>C<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>p<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">h(s)\\le C(p)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand therefore admissibility. The converse does not hold. In the example\ngraph,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>h<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">h<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis consistent: for example,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>4<\/mn><mo>≤<\/mo><mn>3<\/mn><mo>+<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">4\\le 3+2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>2<\/mn><mo>≤<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mo>+<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">2\\le 2+0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe true remaining costs for S, A, B and Z are 5, 5, 2 and 0\nrespectively. Under consistency,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>f<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">f<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ncannot decrease along a path. In a finite graph with non-negative costs,\ncorrect graph search removes a goal optimally; with a merely admissible,\ninconsistent heuristic, previously expanded states must be eligible for\nreopening when a better cost is found. General infinite spaces require\nfurther termination conditions.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Implementation with a cost table<\/h2>\n<div class=\"sourceCode\" id=\"cb1\"><pre\nclass=\"sourceCode python\"><code class=\"sourceCode python\"><span id=\"cb1-1\"><a href=\"#cb1-1\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"im\">from<\/span> heapq <span class=\"im\">import<\/span> heappop, heappush<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-2\"><a href=\"#cb1-2\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>graph <span class=\"op\">=<\/span> {<span class=\"st\">&#39;S&#39;<\/span>:[(<span class=\"st\">&#39;A&#39;<\/span>,<span class=\"dv\">2<\/span>),(<span class=\"st\">&#39;B&#39;<\/span>,<span class=\"dv\">3<\/span>)],<span class=\"st\">&#39;A&#39;<\/span>:[(<span class=\"st\">&#39;Z&#39;<\/span>,<span class=\"dv\">5<\/span>)],<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-3\"><a href=\"#cb1-3\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>         <span class=\"st\">&#39;B&#39;<\/span>:[(<span class=\"st\">&#39;Z&#39;<\/span>,<span class=\"dv\">2<\/span>)], <span class=\"st\">&#39;Z&#39;<\/span>:[]}<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-4\"><a href=\"#cb1-4\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>h <span class=\"op\">=<\/span> <span class=\"bu\">dict<\/span>(S<span class=\"op\">=<\/span><span class=\"dv\">4<\/span>,A<span class=\"op\">=<\/span><span class=\"dv\">4<\/span>,B<span class=\"op\">=<\/span><span class=\"dv\">2<\/span>,Z<span class=\"op\">=<\/span><span class=\"dv\">0<\/span>)<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-5\"><a href=\"#cb1-5\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"cf\">assert<\/span> <span class=\"bu\">all<\/span>(h[s] <span class=\"op\">&lt;=<\/span> c<span class=\"op\">+<\/span>h[t] <span class=\"cf\">for<\/span> s <span class=\"kw\">in<\/span> graph <span class=\"cf\">for<\/span> t,c <span class=\"kw\">in<\/span> graph[s])<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-6\"><a href=\"#cb1-6\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>q <span class=\"op\">=<\/span> [(h[<span class=\"st\">&#39;S&#39;<\/span>],<span class=\"dv\">0<\/span>,<span class=\"st\">&#39;S&#39;<\/span>)]<span class=\"op\">;<\/span> best <span class=\"op\">=<\/span> {<span class=\"st\">&#39;S&#39;<\/span>:<span class=\"dv\">0<\/span>}<span class=\"op\">;<\/span> order <span class=\"op\">=<\/span> []<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-7\"><a href=\"#cb1-7\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"cf\">while<\/span> q:<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-8\"><a href=\"#cb1-8\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>    f,g,s <span class=\"op\">=<\/span> heappop(q)<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-9\"><a href=\"#cb1-9\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>    <span class=\"cf\">if<\/span> g <span class=\"op\">!=<\/span> best[s]:<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-10\"><a href=\"#cb1-10\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>        <span class=\"cf\">continue<\/span><\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-11\"><a href=\"#cb1-11\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>    order.append(s)<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-12\"><a href=\"#cb1-12\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>    <span class=\"cf\">if<\/span> s <span class=\"op\">==<\/span> <span class=\"st\">&#39;Z&#39;<\/span>:<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-13\"><a href=\"#cb1-13\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>        <span class=\"cf\">break<\/span><\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-14\"><a href=\"#cb1-14\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>    <span class=\"cf\">for<\/span> t,c <span class=\"kw\">in<\/span> graph[s]:<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-15\"><a href=\"#cb1-15\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>        <span class=\"cf\">if<\/span> g<span class=\"op\">+<\/span>c <span class=\"op\">&lt;<\/span> best.get(t,<span class=\"bu\">float<\/span>(<span class=\"st\">&#39;inf&#39;<\/span>)):<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-16\"><a href=\"#cb1-16\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>            best[t] <span class=\"op\">=<\/span> g<span class=\"op\">+<\/span>c<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-17\"><a href=\"#cb1-17\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>            heappush(q,(g<span class=\"op\">+<\/span>c<span class=\"op\">+<\/span>h[t],g<span class=\"op\">+<\/span>c,t))<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-18\"><a href=\"#cb1-18\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"cf\">assert<\/span> (g,order) <span class=\"op\">==<\/span> (<span class=\"dv\">5<\/span>,[<span class=\"st\">&#39;S&#39;<\/span>,<span class=\"st\">&#39;B&#39;<\/span>,<span class=\"st\">&#39;Z&#39;<\/span>])<\/span><\/code><\/pre><\/div>\n<p>The table suppresses worse duplicates but does not prevent\nre-expansion when costs improve. Heuristics can be constructed from\nsimplified problems: on a grid with horizontal and vertical unit steps\nand no shortcuts, Manhattan distance provides a lower bound if obstacles\nare ignored. If diagonal movement is permitted, it may be too large. A\nstrong heuristic saves time only if calculating it does not consume the\nbenefit.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Exercise and solution<\/h2>\n<p>Set\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>h<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>S<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mn>5<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mi>h<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>B<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">h(S)=5,h(B)=0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand leave the other values unchanged. Is the new heuristic admissible\nand consistent? Solution: it nowhere exceeds the true remaining costs\nand is thus admissible. But for S→B,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>5<\/mn><mo>&gt;<\/mo><mn>3<\/mn><mo>+<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">5&gt;3+0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nso it is inconsistent. An admissible heuristic is not automatically\nsuitable for an implementation without reopening.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources and further reading<\/h2>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://www.cs.ubc.ca/~poole/aibook/3e/html/ArtInt3e.Ch3.S6.html\">Poole/Mackworth\n(2023): Informed (Heuristic) Search<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> – heuristics and A*; selective\ncross-check. <strong>Verification:<\/strong> pending.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>","university_data-science-and-artificial-intelligence_en:M13-L4":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L4\">M13-L4 – Constraints and constraint satisfaction<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M13 – Bachelor: Symbolic AI and intelligent search (8 lessons)<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Bachelor · Core/elective<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prerequisites:<\/strong> M2, M3, <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_data-science-and-artificial-intelligence&amp;module=M13&amp;lesson=L3&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M13-L3<\/a>.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Learning objective:<\/strong> Formulate variables, domains and\nconstraints, apply arc consistency and check an assignment.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Assignments rather than paths<\/h2>\n<p>A university laboratory schedules three experiments A, B and C in two\ntime slots, 1 and 2. A and B require the same measuring instrument,\nwhile B and C require the same supervisor. Neither pair may take place\nsimultaneously. A constraint satisfaction problem (CSP) consists of\nvariables\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>X<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">X<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\ntheir domains\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>D<\/mi><mi>i<\/mi><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">D_i<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand a set of constraints\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>C<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">C<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe task is to assign values to all variables so that every hard\nconstraint is satisfied. In the example,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>A<\/mi><mo>≠<\/mo><mi>B<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">A\\ne B<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>B<\/mi><mo>≠<\/mo><mi>C<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">B\\ne C<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nInitially this is a feasibility question; a preferred time slot or\nminimised waiting time would be an additional optimisation problem.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Propagation: removing impossible values<\/h2>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-university-data-science-and-artificial-intelligence-en/media/img/001538_en.svg\"\nalt=\"Drei Variablen A, B und C mit Ungleichheitskanten; die Domänen schrumpfen auf 1, 2 und 1.\" />\n<figcaption aria-hidden=\"true\">Drei Variablen A, B und C mit\nUngleichheitskanten; die Domänen schrumpfen auf 1, 2 und 1.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p><em>Figure: Kantenkonsistenz bei zwei Zeitfenstern.<\/em><\/p>\n<p>Assume A is available only in time slot 1. The constraint graph in\nthe illustration has edges A–B and B–C. For each directed arc\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>X<\/mi><mi>i<\/mi><\/msub><mo>→<\/mo><msub><mi>X<\/mi><mi>j<\/mi><\/msub><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">X_i\\to X_j<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\na binary relation is arc-consistent when every remaining value\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>v<\/mi><mo>∈<\/mo><msub><mi>D<\/mi><mi>i<\/mi><\/msub><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">v\\in D_i<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nhas at least one compatible value\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>w<\/mi><mo>∈<\/mo><msub><mi>D<\/mi><mi>j<\/mi><\/msub><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">w\\in D_j<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>:<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo>∀<\/mo><mi>v<\/mi><mo>∈<\/mo><msub><mi>D<\/mi><mi>i<\/mi><\/msub><mspace width=\"0.222em\"><\/mspace><mo>∃<\/mo><mi>w<\/mi><mo>∈<\/mo><msub><mi>D<\/mi><mi>j<\/mi><\/msub><mo>:<\/mo><mspace width=\"0.222em\"><\/mspace><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>v<\/mi><mo>,<\/mo><mi>w<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>∈<\/mo><msub><mi>R<\/mi><mrow><mi>i<\/mi><mi>j<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\forall v\\in D_i\\ \\exists w\\in D_j:\\ (v,w)\\in R_{ij}.<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>From\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>D<\/mi><mi>A<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">{<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">}<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">D_A=\\{1\\}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nit follows that B cannot be 1. From\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>D<\/mi><mi>B<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">{<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">}<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">D_B=\\{2\\}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nit follows that C cannot be 2. The domains become\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">{<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">}<\/mo><mo>,<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">{<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">}<\/mo><mo>,<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">{<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">}<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\{1\\},\\{2\\},\\{1\\}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nMethods such as AC-3 recheck affected arcs after changes until no\nfurther value can be removed or an empty domain indicates a\ncontradiction. Arc consistency does not prove that a global solution\nexists in a general graph. For example, three pairwise distinct\nvariables with domains\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">{<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">}<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\{1,2\\}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nremain arc-consistent but cannot be solved jointly.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Search with backtracking<\/h2>\n<p>Backtracking extends a partial assignment, checks all constraints\nthat can already be evaluated and discards a branch as soon as a\nconflict arises. MRV (minimum remaining values) preferentially selects\nthe variable with the smallest remaining domain. Bottlenecks are thus\nrecognised early. When domains are equally small, the variable with\nparticularly many unassigned neighbours may take priority.<\/p>\n<div class=\"sourceCode\" id=\"cb1\"><pre\nclass=\"sourceCode python\"><code class=\"sourceCode python\"><span id=\"cb1-1\"><a href=\"#cb1-1\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"im\">from<\/span> itertools <span class=\"im\">import<\/span> product<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-2\"><a href=\"#cb1-2\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"kw\">def<\/span> feasible(domains, edges):<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-3\"><a href=\"#cb1-3\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>    <span class=\"cf\">return<\/span> [p <span class=\"cf\">for<\/span> p <span class=\"kw\">in<\/span> product(<span class=\"op\">*<\/span>domains)<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-4\"><a href=\"#cb1-4\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>            <span class=\"cf\">if<\/span> <span class=\"bu\">all<\/span>(p[i] <span class=\"op\">!=<\/span> p[j] <span class=\"cf\">for<\/span> i,j <span class=\"kw\">in<\/span> edges)]<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-5\"><a href=\"#cb1-5\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"cf\">assert<\/span> feasible([[<span class=\"dv\">1<\/span>],[<span class=\"dv\">1<\/span>,<span class=\"dv\">2<\/span>],[<span class=\"dv\">1<\/span>,<span class=\"dv\">2<\/span>]],[(<span class=\"dv\">0<\/span>,<span class=\"dv\">1<\/span>),(<span class=\"dv\">1<\/span>,<span class=\"dv\">2<\/span>)]) <span class=\"op\">==<\/span> [(<span class=\"dv\">1<\/span>,<span class=\"dv\">2<\/span>,<span class=\"dv\">1<\/span>)]<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-6\"><a href=\"#cb1-6\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"cf\">assert<\/span> feasible([[<span class=\"dv\">1<\/span>,<span class=\"dv\">2<\/span>]]<span class=\"op\">*<\/span><span class=\"dv\">3<\/span>,[(<span class=\"dv\">0<\/span>,<span class=\"dv\">1<\/span>),(<span class=\"dv\">1<\/span>,<span class=\"dv\">2<\/span>),(<span class=\"dv\">0<\/span>,<span class=\"dv\">2<\/span>)]) <span class=\"op\">==<\/span> []<\/span><\/code><\/pre><\/div>\n<p>Enumeration tests at most\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mn>2<\/mn><mn>3<\/mn><\/msup><mo>=<\/mo><mn>8<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">2^3=8<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ncomplete assignments when all domains contain both values. Propagation\ncan rule out many of these combinations without generating them in full.\nIndependent subgraphs may be solved separately, whereas global\nconstraints such as AllDifferent often benefit from specialised\npropagators. An empty solution space may also indicate incorrectly\nrecorded availability; therefore document the origin of each hard\nconstraint.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Exercise and solution<\/h2>\n<p>A and C must now also occupy different time slots. Can a third slot\nsolve the problem? Solution: yes, for example A=1, B=2 and C=3, provided\nB and C are actually allowed to use the third slot. With two slots the\ntriangular structure is unsatisfiable. An infeasible assignment must not\nsilently be presented as an optimal solution.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources and further reading<\/h2>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://www.cs.ubc.ca/~poole/aibook/3e/html/ArtInt3e.Ch4.S3.html\">Poole/Mackworth\n(2023): Consistency Algorithms<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> – arc consistency; selective\ncross-check. <strong>Verification:<\/strong> pending.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>","university_data-science-and-artificial-intelligence_en:M13-L5":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L5\">M13-L5 – Propositional and predicate logic in AI systems<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M13 – Bachelor: Symbolic AI and intelligent search (8 lessons)<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Bachelor · Core/elective<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prerequisites:<\/strong> M2, M3, <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_data-science-and-artificial-intelligence&amp;module=M13&amp;lesson=L4&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M13-L4<\/a>.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Learning objective:<\/strong> Formalise propositions and\npredicates, distinguish models from logical entailment and interpret\nquantifiers.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Truth and models<\/h2>\n<p>A controller may start a machine if authorisation F is present and\nsafety contact K is closed. In the model, F, K and S (start permitted)\ndenote Boolean propositions. The rule might be\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>S<\/mi><mo>↔<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>F<\/mi><mo>∧<\/mo><mi>K<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">S\\leftrightarrow(F\\land K)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThis does not assert that the machine is actually running, but only\nspecifies when permission is granted within the model. Propositional\nlogic has truth values, not probabilities. Negation\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mo>¬<\/mo><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\neg<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nconjunction\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>∧<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\land<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\ndisjunction\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>∨<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\lor<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand implication\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mo>→<\/mo><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\to<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nare defined by their truth conditions. An implication\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>P<\/mi><mo>→<\/mo><mi>Q<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">P\\to Q<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis false precisely when P is true and Q is false. It asserts neither\ntemporal order nor causality.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Entailment rather than conjecture<\/h2>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-university-data-science-and-artificial-intelligence-en/media/img/001628_en.svg\"\nalt=\"Vier Belegungen von Freigabe und Kontakt; der Start ist nur bei zwei wahren Eingaben erlaubt.\" />\n<figcaption aria-hidden=\"true\">Vier Belegungen von Freigabe und Kontakt;\nder Start ist nur bei zwei wahren Eingaben erlaubt.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p><em>Figure: Wahrheitstabelle einer Freigaberegel.<\/em><\/p>\n<p>In a knowledge base K (written here as\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi mathvariant=\"script\">𝒦<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mathcal K<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nto distinguish it from the safety contact),\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>φ<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\varphi<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis logically entailed if every model of the base also satisfies\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>φ<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\varphi<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>:<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"script\">𝒦<\/mi><mo>⊨<\/mo><mi>φ<\/mi><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mathcal K\\models\\varphi.<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>In the teaching example, authorisation and a closed contact entail\npermission. Conversely, missing authorisation alone does not imply that\nthe contact is open. The illustration shows the four input combinations\nand the resulting value of S. A formula is satisfiable if at least one\nassignment makes it true, valid if this holds for all assignments, and\nunsatisfiable if no satisfying assignment exists. An inconsistent\nknowledge base has no model; under classical entailment every formula\nfollows from it. Consistency checks are therefore essential.<\/p>\n<div class=\"sourceCode\" id=\"cb1\"><pre\nclass=\"sourceCode python\"><code class=\"sourceCode python\"><span id=\"cb1-1\"><a href=\"#cb1-1\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"im\">from<\/span> itertools <span class=\"im\">import<\/span> product<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-2\"><a href=\"#cb1-2\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>models <span class=\"op\">=<\/span> [(f,k,s) <span class=\"cf\">for<\/span> f,k,s <span class=\"kw\">in<\/span> product([<span class=\"va\">False<\/span>,<span class=\"va\">True<\/span>],repeat<span class=\"op\">=<\/span><span class=\"dv\">3<\/span>)<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-3\"><a href=\"#cb1-3\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>          <span class=\"cf\">if<\/span> s <span class=\"op\">==<\/span> (f <span class=\"kw\">and<\/span> k)]<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-4\"><a href=\"#cb1-4\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"cf\">assert<\/span> <span class=\"bu\">len<\/span>(models) <span class=\"op\">==<\/span> <span class=\"dv\">4<\/span><\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-5\"><a href=\"#cb1-5\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"cf\">assert<\/span> <span class=\"bu\">all<\/span>(s <span class=\"cf\">for<\/span> f,k,s <span class=\"kw\">in<\/span> models <span class=\"cf\">if<\/span> f <span class=\"kw\">and<\/span> k)<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-6\"><a href=\"#cb1-6\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"cf\">assert<\/span> <span class=\"bu\">any<\/span>(k <span class=\"cf\">for<\/span> f,k,s <span class=\"kw\">in<\/span> models <span class=\"cf\">if<\/span> <span class=\"kw\">not<\/span> f)<\/span><\/code><\/pre><\/div>\n<p>Checking by complete enumeration requires up to\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msup><mn>2<\/mn><mi>n<\/mi><\/msup><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">2^n<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nassignments for\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>n<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">n<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nindependent propositions. Rules of inference often allow direct\nderivations, for example deriving Q from P and\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>P<\/mi><mo>→<\/mo><mi>Q<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">P\\to Q<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nby modus ponens.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>From propositions to individuals<\/h2>\n<p>Predicate logic additionally describes objects and relations. Instead\nof inventing a separate proposition for each device, we can define a\nunary predicate\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">b<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d<\/mi><\/mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mathrm{Calibrated}(x)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand a binary predicate\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">U<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><\/mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo>,<\/mo><mi>y<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mathrm{Uses}(x,y)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nfor example. A rule for all devices might be:<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo>∀<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mspace width=\"0.222em\"><\/mspace><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">b<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d<\/mi><\/mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>→<\/mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">h<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">c<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">k<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d<\/mi><\/mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\forall x\\ (\\mathrm{Calibrated}(x)\\to \\mathrm{Checked}(x)).<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>Together with\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">b<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d<\/mi><\/mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>a<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mathrm{Calibrated}(a)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nthis entails\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">h<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">c<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">k<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d<\/mi><\/mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>a<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mathrm{Checked}(a)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nBy contrast,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo>∃<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mspace width=\"0.222em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">h<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">c<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">k<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d<\/mi><\/mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\exists x\\ \\mathrm{Checked}(x)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ndenotes the existence of at least one checked object without determining\nits identity. Negating a universal statement asserts the existence of a\ncounterexample. Quantifier order is crucial:\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo>∀<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo>∃<\/mo><mi>y<\/mi><mspace width=\"0.222em\"><\/mspace><mi>R<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo>,<\/mo><mi>y<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\forall x\\exists y\\ R(x,y)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nallows different partners y, whereas\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo>∃<\/mo><mi>y<\/mi><mo>∀<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mspace width=\"0.222em\"><\/mspace><mi>R<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo>,<\/mo><mi>y<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\exists y\\forall x\\ R(x,y)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nrequires a common partner. Full first-order predicate logic is\nundecidable in general; specialised fragments are considered in <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_data-science-and-artificial-intelligence&amp;module=M13&amp;lesson=L6&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M13-L6<\/a>\nand <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_data-science-and-artificial-intelligence&amp;module=M13&amp;lesson=L8&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M13-L8<\/a>.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Exercise and solution<\/h2>\n<p>We wish to infer P from\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>P<\/mi><mo>→<\/mo><mi>Q<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">P\\to Q<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand Q. Is this valid? Solution: no. The assignment P=false, Q=true\nsatisfies both premises but not P. What is the negation of\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo>∀<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mspace width=\"0.222em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">h<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">c<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">k<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d<\/mi><\/mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\forall x\\ \\mathrm{Checked}(x)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>?\nSolution:\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo>∃<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mspace width=\"0.222em\"><\/mspace><mo>¬<\/mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">h<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">c<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">k<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d<\/mi><\/mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\exists x\\ \\neg \\mathrm{Checked}(x)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nAt least one object has not been checked.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources and further reading<\/h2>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://www.cs.ubc.ca/~poole/aibook/3e/html/ArtInt3e.Ch5.html\">Poole/Mackworth\n(2023): Propositions and Inference<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> – chapter overview; propositional\nlogic; selective cross-check. <strong>Verification:<\/strong>\npending.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://www.cs.ubc.ca/~poole/aibook/3e/html/ArtInt3e.Ch5.S3.html\">Poole/Mackworth\n(2023): Propositional Definite Clauses<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> – rule-based inference;\nselective cross-check. <strong>Verification:<\/strong> pending.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://www.cs.ubc.ca/~poole/aibook/3e/html/ArtInt3e.Ch15.S3.html\">Poole/Mackworth\n(2023): Predicate Calculus<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> – quantifiers and semantics; selective\ncross-check. <strong>Verification:<\/strong> pending.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>","university_data-science-and-artificial-intelligence_en:M13-L6":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L6\">M13-L6 – Knowledge representation, ontologies and inference<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M13 – Bachelor: Symbolic AI and intelligent search (8 lessons)<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Bachelor · Core/elective<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prerequisites:<\/strong> M2, M3, <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_data-science-and-artificial-intelligence&amp;module=M13&amp;lesson=L5&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M13-L5<\/a>.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Learning objective:<\/strong> Model knowledge as facts, rules\nand an ontology, and distinguish unknown from false propositions.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Defining concepts and relationships<\/h2>\n<p>A research database records measuring instruments, sensors and\nlaboratory rooms. A knowledge graph can connect individual objects via\ntyped edges: sensor s7 is located in room r2. An ontology additionally\nspecifies the intended concepts and relations and their logical\nproperties. For example, Sensor may be a subclass of\nMeasuringInstrument. Every object classified as a sensor then also\nbelongs to the superclass. A graph alone does not guarantee this\ninference; it depends on the defined axioms and the semantics used.<\/p>\n<p>In the Resource Description Framework (RDF), statements are organised\nas triples (subject, predicate, object). OWL 2 additionally allows\nformal class relationships and properties. A class describes a set of\npossible instances, while an individual is a specific object. Properties\nconnect individuals or assign data values to them. When modelling,\ndistinguish whether an expression denotes a category, a particular\ndevice or a measured value.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Applying rules and recording derivations<\/h2>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-university-data-science-and-artificial-intelligence-en/media/img/001736_en.svg\"\nalt=\"Aus Sensor(s7) und zwei Unterklassenbeziehungen folgen Messgerät(s7) und Inventarobjekt(s7).\" />\n<figcaption aria-hidden=\"true\">Aus Sensor(s7) und zwei\nUnterklassenbeziehungen folgen Messgerät(s7) und\nInventarobjekt(s7).<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p><em>Figure: Vom expliziten Fakt zur Folgerung.<\/em><\/p>\n<p>Our original teaching example uses the facts Sensor(s7), Ort(s7,r2)\n(location), and the rules\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">S<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r<\/mi><\/mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>→<\/mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">M<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">g<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t<\/mi><\/mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mathrm{Sensor}(x)\\to \\mathrm{Messgeraet}(x)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">M<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">g<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t<\/mi><\/mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>→<\/mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">I<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">v<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">b<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">j<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">k<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t<\/mi><\/mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mathrm{Messgeraet}(x)\\to \\mathrm{Inventarobjekt}(x)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\neach implicitly universally quantified. Thus Inventarobjekt(s7)\n(inventory item) can be derived. The illustration distinguishes the\nexplicit type assertion from the two inferred types. The identifiers\nMessgeraet (measuring instrument) and Inventarobjekt (inventory item)\nare retained in the rules and code.<\/p>\n<p>Forward chaining starts with known facts and adds conclusions until\nnothing changes. Backward chaining starts with the query and looks for\nrules and facts that support it. For definite Horn rules over a finite\nset of ground atoms, forward chaining can proceed to a fixed point. The\nfollowing example, restricted to a single individual, implements exactly\nthis principle; it is not a general OWL reasoner.<\/p>\n<div class=\"sourceCode\" id=\"cb1\"><pre\nclass=\"sourceCode python\"><code class=\"sourceCode python\"><span id=\"cb1-1\"><a href=\"#cb1-1\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>facts <span class=\"op\">=<\/span> {<span class=\"st\">&#39;Sensor(s7)&#39;<\/span>,<span class=\"st\">&#39;Ort(s7,r2)&#39;<\/span>}<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-2\"><a href=\"#cb1-2\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>rules <span class=\"op\">=<\/span> [(<span class=\"st\">&#39;Messgeraet(s7)&#39;<\/span>,<span class=\"st\">&#39;Inventarobjekt(s7)&#39;<\/span>),<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-3\"><a href=\"#cb1-3\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>         (<span class=\"st\">&#39;Sensor(s7)&#39;<\/span>,<span class=\"st\">&#39;Messgeraet(s7)&#39;<\/span>)]<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-4\"><a href=\"#cb1-4\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"cf\">while<\/span> <span class=\"va\">True<\/span>:<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-5\"><a href=\"#cb1-5\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>    new <span class=\"op\">=<\/span> facts <span class=\"op\">|<\/span> {b <span class=\"cf\">for<\/span> a,b <span class=\"kw\">in<\/span> rules <span class=\"cf\">if<\/span> a <span class=\"kw\">in<\/span> facts}<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-6\"><a href=\"#cb1-6\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>    <span class=\"cf\">if<\/span> new <span class=\"op\">==<\/span> facts:<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-7\"><a href=\"#cb1-7\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>        <span class=\"cf\">break<\/span><\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-8\"><a href=\"#cb1-8\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>    facts <span class=\"op\">=<\/span> new<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-9\"><a href=\"#cb1-9\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"cf\">assert<\/span> <span class=\"st\">&#39;Inventarobjekt(s7)&#39;<\/span> <span class=\"kw\">in<\/span> facts<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-10\"><a href=\"#cb1-10\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"cf\">assert<\/span> <span class=\"bu\">len<\/span>(facts) <span class=\"op\">==<\/span> <span class=\"dv\">4<\/span><\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-11\"><a href=\"#cb1-11\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"cf\">assert<\/span> <span class=\"st\">&#39;Sensor(r2)&#39;<\/span> <span class=\"kw\">not<\/span> <span class=\"kw\">in<\/span> facts<\/span><\/code><\/pre><\/div>\n<p>Here, the order of the rules affects only the number of iterations\nrequired to reach the fixed point. The derivation can be logged using\nrule and fact identifiers. This facilitates diagnosis of incorrect\nconclusions but does not replace checking the underlying facts.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Open world and identity<\/h2>\n<p>If information about whether s7 is calibrated is missing, this does\nnot mean that s7 is uncalibrated under the OWL open-world assumption.\nThis differs from a database query that returns only existing entries.\nDifferent names in OWL also do not automatically denote different\nindividuals; equality or inequality may need to be expressed. An\ninventory check that treats missing calibration data as an error should\ntherefore be modelled as a separate validation rule. Ontological\nreasoning and completeness checking pursue different goals. For large\ngraphs, restricted language profiles, indexing and incremental updates\ncan help; more expressive modelling may require more computation\ntime.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Exercise and solution<\/h2>\n<p>The ontology declares Sensor and Room to be disjoint classes. s7 is\nassigned to both. What happens? Solution: under classical OWL semantics\nthe knowledge base is inconsistent. The disjoint classes cannot have a\ncommon individual. If only the assertion Room(s7) (Raum(s7) in the\noriginal notation) is missing, however, without additional axioms its\nabsence alone does not imply that it is false.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources and further reading<\/h2>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://www.cs.ubc.ca/~poole/aibook/3e/html/ArtInt3e.Ch5.S3.html\">Poole/Mackworth\n(2023): Propositional Definite Clauses<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> – rule-based inference;\nselective cross-check. <strong>Verification:<\/strong> pending.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"https://www.w3.org/TR/owl2-primer/\">W3C: OWL 2 Web Ontology\nLanguage Primer (Second Edition)<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> – classes, properties, open world;\nselective cross-check. <strong>Verification:<\/strong> pending.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>","university_data-science-and-artificial-intelligence_en:M13-L7":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L7\">M13-L7 – Automated planning and adversarial search<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M13 – Bachelor: Symbolic AI and intelligent search (8 lessons)<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Bachelor · Core/elective<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prerequisites:<\/strong> M2, M3, <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_data-science-and-artificial-intelligence&amp;module=M13&amp;lesson=L6&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M13-L6<\/a>.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Learning objective:<\/strong> Model deterministic action\nplanning and minimax search and calculate their different objective\nquantities.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Plans as sequences of executable actions<\/h2>\n<p>An automated laboratory system must transport a sample from a storage\nmagazine to an analyser. A state can be represented as a set of true\natomic facts. In the simplified model, bereit (ready) means that the\nsample is accessible, geladen (loaded) that it is inside the device, and\ngemessen (measured) that measurement has taken place. The Load action\nrequires bereit, adds geladen and removes bereit. Measure requires\ngeladen and adds gemessen. An action is applicable only when its\npreconditions are satisfied.<\/p>\n<p>For positive preconditions and sets of atoms to be added or removed,\nthe simplified STRIPS transition rule is<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>s<\/mi><mo>′<\/mo><\/msup><mo>=<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>s<\/mi><mo>\\<\/mo><mi>D<\/mi><mi>e<\/mi><mi>l<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>a<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>∪<\/mo><mi>A<\/mi><mi>d<\/mi><mi>d<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>a<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>,<\/mo><mspace width=\"2.0em\"><\/mspace><mi>P<\/mi><mi>r<\/mi><mi>e<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>a<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>⊆<\/mo><mi>s<\/mi><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">s&#39;=(s\\setminus Del(a))\\cup Add(a),\\qquad Pre(a)\\subseteq s.<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>The planning task is to find an action sequence that takes the\ninitial state\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>s<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">{<\/mo><mi>b<\/mi><mi>e<\/mi><mi>r<\/mi><mi>e<\/mi><mi>i<\/mi><mi>t<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">}<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">s_0=\\{bereit\\}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nto a state containing gemessen. All unchanged facts are retained in this\nmodel. Forward search examines applicable actions; backward planning\nregresses goal conditions through suitable actions. Parallelism,\ndurations, resource consumption and uncertain effects require more\nextensive models. Correct planning within the model does not yet\nguarantee physical executability.<\/p>\n<div class=\"sourceCode\" id=\"cb1\"><pre\nclass=\"sourceCode python\"><code class=\"sourceCode python\"><span id=\"cb1-1\"><a href=\"#cb1-1\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>s <span class=\"op\">=<\/span> {<span class=\"st\">&#39;bereit&#39;<\/span>}<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-2\"><a href=\"#cb1-2\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>actions <span class=\"op\">=<\/span> [({<span class=\"st\">&#39;bereit&#39;<\/span>},{<span class=\"st\">&#39;geladen&#39;<\/span>},{<span class=\"st\">&#39;bereit&#39;<\/span>}),<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-3\"><a href=\"#cb1-3\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>           ({<span class=\"st\">&#39;geladen&#39;<\/span>},{<span class=\"st\">&#39;gemessen&#39;<\/span>},<span class=\"bu\">set<\/span>())]<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-4\"><a href=\"#cb1-4\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"cf\">for<\/span> pre,add,delete <span class=\"kw\">in<\/span> actions:<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-5\"><a href=\"#cb1-5\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>    <span class=\"cf\">assert<\/span> pre <span class=\"op\">&lt;=<\/span> s<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-6\"><a href=\"#cb1-6\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>    s <span class=\"op\">=<\/span> (s<span class=\"op\">-<\/span>delete)<span class=\"op\">|<\/span>add<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-7\"><a href=\"#cb1-7\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"cf\">assert<\/span> s <span class=\"op\">==<\/span> {<span class=\"st\">&#39;geladen&#39;<\/span>,<span class=\"st\">&#39;gemessen&#39;<\/span>}<\/span><\/code><\/pre><\/div>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Opponents change the choice<\/h2>\n<p>In a deterministic two-player zero-sum game with perfect information,\nthe consequences of a choice also depend on the opponent’s moves. MAX\nwants a high utility, MIN a low one. Instead of finding one favourable\npath, the agent must evaluate a decision against all admissible optimal\nresponses. For terminal states,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>V<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>s<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mi>U<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>s<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">V(s)=U(s)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>;\nat MAX and MIN turns respectively,<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>V<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>s<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><munder><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">max<\/mi><mrow><mi>t<\/mi><mo>∈<\/mo><mi>S<\/mi><mi>u<\/mi><mi>c<\/mi><mi>c<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>s<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><\/munder><mi>V<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>t<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mspace width=\"1.0em\"><\/mspace><mtext mathvariant=\"normal\">or<\/mtext><mspace width=\"1.0em\"><\/mspace><mi>V<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>s<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><munder><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">min<\/mi><mrow><mi>t<\/mi><mo>∈<\/mo><mi>S<\/mi><mi>u<\/mi><mi>c<\/mi><mi>c<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>s<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><\/munder><mi>V<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>t<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">V(s)=\\max_{t\\in Succ(s)}V(t)\\quad\\text{or}\\quad V(s)=\\min_{t\\in Succ(s)}V(t).<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>In the illustrated teaching tree, MAX may choose left or right. On\nthe left, MIN may choose between utilities of 6 and 8 points; on the\nright, between 4 and 11. The subtree values are 6 and 4, and the root\nreceives 6. The attractive terminal value 11 is not guaranteed, because\nMIN can prefer 4. Utility points are model values, not\nprobabilities.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Alpha-beta and search limits<\/h2>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-university-data-science-and-artificial-intelligence-en/media/img/001648_en.svg\"\nalt=\"MAX wählt links mit garantiertem Nutzen 6; rechts reicht das Blatt 4, um das Blatt 11 abzuschneiden.\" />\n<figcaption aria-hidden=\"true\">MAX wählt links mit garantiertem Nutzen\n6; rechts reicht das Blatt 4, um das Blatt 11\nabzuschneiden.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p><em>Figure: Minimax und Alpha-Beta-Schnitt.<\/em><\/p>\n<p>After evaluating the left subtree, MAX has the lower bound\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>α<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>6<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\alpha=6<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nIn the right subtree the first leaf yields 4, so this MIN node already\nhas an upper bound\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>β<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>4<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\beta=4<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nAs soon as\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>α<\/mi><mo>≥<\/mo><mi>β<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\alpha\\ge\\beta<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nthe remaining right branch can be omitted without changing the optimal\nroot value. Such a cut does not necessarily reveal the exact value of\nthe pruned subtree. For large trees, a depth limit with estimated state\nvalues is often used as well; this forfeits the general guarantee of\noptimal moves.<\/p>\n<div class=\"sourceCode\" id=\"cb2\"><pre\nclass=\"sourceCode python\"><code class=\"sourceCode python\"><span id=\"cb2-1\"><a href=\"#cb2-1\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>scores <span class=\"op\">=<\/span> [[<span class=\"dv\">6<\/span>,<span class=\"dv\">8<\/span>],[<span class=\"dv\">4<\/span>,<span class=\"dv\">11<\/span>]]<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb2-2\"><a href=\"#cb2-2\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"cf\">assert<\/span> <span class=\"bu\">max<\/span>(<span class=\"bu\">map<\/span>(<span class=\"bu\">min<\/span>,scores)) <span class=\"op\">==<\/span> <span class=\"dv\">6<\/span><\/span>\n<span id=\"cb2-3\"><a href=\"#cb2-3\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>alpha <span class=\"op\">=<\/span> <span class=\"bu\">min<\/span>(scores[<span class=\"dv\">0<\/span>])<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb2-4\"><a href=\"#cb2-4\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>beta <span class=\"op\">=<\/span> scores[<span class=\"dv\">1<\/span>][<span class=\"dv\">0<\/span>]<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb2-5\"><a href=\"#cb2-5\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"cf\">assert<\/span> alpha <span class=\"op\">&gt;=<\/span> beta<\/span><\/code><\/pre><\/div>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Exercise and solution<\/h2>\n<p>The first right leaf value increases from 4 to 7. Can the second\nright leaf value still be skipped? Solution: no, 7 is greater than the\ncurrent bound of 6. With the second value 11, the right subtree yields 7\nand MAX chooses right. What confusion would be problematic with a random\nrather than deliberately minimising opponent? The minimum is then not\nthe expected value of a probability distribution; another decision model\nwould be needed.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources and further reading<\/h2>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://www.cs.ubc.ca/~poole/aibook/3e/html/ArtInt3e.Ch6.S1.html\">Poole/Mackworth\n(2023): Representing States, Actions, and Goals<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> – planning model;\nselective cross-check. <strong>Verification:<\/strong> pending.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://inst.eecs.berkeley.edu/~cs188/textbook/games/minimax.html\">UC\nBerkeley CS188: Minimax<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> – minimax and alpha-beta; selective\ncross-check. <strong>Verification:<\/strong> pending.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>","university_data-science-and-artificial-intelligence_en:M13-L8":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L8\">M13-L8 – Automated reasoning: SAT, SMT and satisfiability checking<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M13 – Bachelor: Symbolic AI and intelligent search (8 lessons)<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Bachelor · Advanced elective<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prerequisites:<\/strong> M2, M3, <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_data-science-and-artificial-intelligence&amp;module=M13&amp;lesson=L7&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M13-L7<\/a>.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Learning objective:<\/strong> Understand SAT and SMT as\nfeasibility checks and analyse CNF and a small arithmetic\ncontradiction.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Boolean satisfiability<\/h2>\n<p>A computer is to operate with two redundant services A and B. At\nleast one must be active; both must not run at the same time because\nthey require exclusive access to a resource. The Boolean specification\nis<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>F<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>A<\/mi><mo>∨<\/mo><mi>B<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>∧<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mo>¬<\/mo><mi>A<\/mi><mo>∨<\/mo><mo>¬<\/mo><mi>B<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">F=(A\\lor B)\\land(\\neg A\\lor\\neg B).<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>A formula in conjunctive normal form (CNF) is a conjunction of\nclauses, each clause consisting of a disjunction of literals (variables\nor their negations). The teaching example has two satisfying models: A\ntrue, B false, and A false, B true. SAT asks whether such a model exists\nat all. SAT is NP-complete; this does not imply that every practical\ninstance is difficult to solve.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Propagation and conflicts<\/h2>\n<p>If the unit clause A is added, the second clause forces\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo>¬<\/mo><mi>B<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\neg B<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nIf B is also added, a conflict arises. In unit propagation, a clause\nwith only one remaining possibly true literal determines its value. The\nDPLL algorithm combines this simplification with branching and\nbacktracking. Modern CDCL solvers learn new implied clauses from\nconflicts to avoid similar fruitless search branches. This can provide\nsubstantial improvements over complete enumeration. A SAT result can be\nchecked against the assignment found; for UNSAT, an appropriate proof\ncertificate can allow independent verification.<\/p>\n<div class=\"sourceCode\" id=\"cb1\"><pre\nclass=\"sourceCode python\"><code class=\"sourceCode python\"><span id=\"cb1-1\"><a href=\"#cb1-1\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"im\">from<\/span> itertools <span class=\"im\">import<\/span> product<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-2\"><a href=\"#cb1-2\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>models <span class=\"op\">=<\/span> [(a,b) <span class=\"cf\">for<\/span> a,b <span class=\"kw\">in<\/span> product([<span class=\"va\">False<\/span>,<span class=\"va\">True<\/span>],repeat<span class=\"op\">=<\/span><span class=\"dv\">2<\/span>)<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-3\"><a href=\"#cb1-3\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>          <span class=\"cf\">if<\/span> (a <span class=\"kw\">or<\/span> b) <span class=\"kw\">and<\/span> <span class=\"kw\">not<\/span>(a <span class=\"kw\">and<\/span> b)]<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-4\"><a href=\"#cb1-4\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"cf\">assert<\/span> models <span class=\"op\">==<\/span> [(<span class=\"va\">False<\/span>,<span class=\"va\">True<\/span>),(<span class=\"va\">True<\/span>,<span class=\"va\">False<\/span>)]<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-5\"><a href=\"#cb1-5\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"cf\">assert<\/span> <span class=\"kw\">not<\/span> <span class=\"bu\">any<\/span>(a <span class=\"kw\">and<\/span> b <span class=\"cf\">for<\/span> a,b <span class=\"kw\">in<\/span> models)<\/span><\/code><\/pre><\/div>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>SMT: constraints over numbers<\/h2>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-university-data-science-and-artificial-intelligence-en/media/img/001705_en.svg\"\nalt=\"Die drei Gitterpunkte (3,5), (3,6), (4,6) erfüllen sämtliche linearen Ganzzahlbedingungen.\" />\n<figcaption aria-hidden=\"true\">Die drei Gitterpunkte (3,5), (3,6), (4,6)\nerfüllen sämtliche linearen Ganzzahlbedingungen.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p><em>Figure: Ganzzahlige Modelle eines SMT-Problems.<\/em><\/p>\n<p>Another teaching example describes two integer start times x and y in\nhours. A two-hour operation must begin and finish before the next one,\nand the second time slot must start no later than hour 6. The conditions\nare\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>x<\/mi><mo>≥<\/mo><mn>3<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x\\ge3<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>y<\/mi><mo>≥<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo>+<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">y\\ge x+2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>y<\/mi><mo>≤<\/mo><mn>6<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">y\\le6<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThey are jointly satisfiable, for example with x=3, y=5. The\nillustration shows precisely the three admissible integer points (3,5),\n(3,6), (4,6). The conditions must all hold in the same interpretation.\nWith\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>y<\/mi><mo>≤<\/mo><mn>4<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">y\\le4<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nno model would be possible, because\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>x<\/mi><mo>≥<\/mo><mn>3<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x\\ge3<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>y<\/mi><mo>≥<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo>+<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">y\\ge x+2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nalready force\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>y<\/mi><mo>≥<\/mo><mn>5<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">y\\ge5<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/p>\n<p>SMT extends the satisfiability question to defined theories (for\nexample linear integer arithmetic, bit vectors or arrays). In SMT-LIB\nthe satisfiable case can be expressed as follows:<\/p>\n<pre class=\"smtlib\"><code>(set-logic QF_LIA)\n(declare-const x Int)\n(declare-const y Int)\n(assert (&gt;= x 3))\n(assert (&gt;= y (+ x 2)))\n(assert (&lt;= y 6))\n(check-sat)<\/code><\/pre>\n<p>The expected result is <code>sat<\/code>. The logic QF_LIA allows\nquantifier-free linear integer arithmetic. The following Python lines\nindependently check this specific small range of values; they do not\nreplace an SMT solver and are not a general decision procedure.<\/p>\n<div class=\"sourceCode\" id=\"cb3\"><pre\nclass=\"sourceCode python\"><code class=\"sourceCode python\"><span id=\"cb3-1\"><a href=\"#cb3-1\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>points <span class=\"op\">=<\/span> [(x,y) <span class=\"cf\">for<\/span> x <span class=\"kw\">in<\/span> <span class=\"bu\">range<\/span>(<span class=\"dv\">3<\/span>,<span class=\"dv\">5<\/span>) <span class=\"cf\">for<\/span> y <span class=\"kw\">in<\/span> <span class=\"bu\">range<\/span>(<span class=\"dv\">5<\/span>,<span class=\"dv\">7<\/span>)<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb3-2\"><a href=\"#cb3-2\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>          <span class=\"cf\">if<\/span> y <span class=\"op\">&gt;=<\/span> x<span class=\"op\">+<\/span><span class=\"dv\">2<\/span>]<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb3-3\"><a href=\"#cb3-3\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"cf\">assert<\/span> points <span class=\"op\">==<\/span> [(<span class=\"dv\">3<\/span>,<span class=\"dv\">5<\/span>),(<span class=\"dv\">3<\/span>,<span class=\"dv\">6<\/span>),(<span class=\"dv\">4<\/span>,<span class=\"dv\">6<\/span>)]<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb3-4\"><a href=\"#cb3-4\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"cf\">assert<\/span> <span class=\"kw\">not<\/span> <span class=\"bu\">any<\/span>(y <span class=\"op\">&gt;=<\/span> x<span class=\"op\">+<\/span><span class=\"dv\">2<\/span> <span class=\"cf\">for<\/span> x <span class=\"kw\">in<\/span> <span class=\"bu\">range<\/span>(<span class=\"dv\">3<\/span>,<span class=\"dv\">5<\/span>) <span class=\"cf\">for<\/span> y <span class=\"kw\">in<\/span> <span class=\"bu\">range<\/span>(<span class=\"dv\">0<\/span>,<span class=\"dv\">5<\/span>))<\/span><\/code><\/pre><\/div>\n<p>For each of the two bounds, the admissible range follows from the\ninequalities; the point is not to restrict an arbitrary SMT instance to\nsmall numbers. Depending on the fragment and implementation, solvers may\nalso report <code>unknown<\/code>. This answer must not be treated as\nUNSAT.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Exercise and solution<\/h2>\n<p>Which models remain in the numerical example with the additional\ncondition\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>y<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>6<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">y=6<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>?\nSolution: (3,6) and (4,6) remain. Does a satisfiable formula prove that\nthe modelled workflow is safe in reality? No, it demonstrates only the\nexistence of a model with respect to the specified conditions. Missing\nresource or safety rules must first be modelled.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources and further reading<\/h2>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://cvc5.github.io/tutorials/beginners/theories.html\">cvc5:\nSatisfiability Modulo Theories – SMT Theories<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> – Boolean and\narithmetic theories; selective cross-check.\n<strong>Verification:<\/strong> pending.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>"};
